Q: How to get i18n JSON file inside packages in monorepo structure? A: <p>I think you will want to utilize the <a href="https://classic.yarnpkg.com/lang/en/docs/workspaces/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">workspace feature in yarn</a>.</p>
<p>Basically you need to declare your workspaces properly for your monorepo, I have an example config below for a package.json for your monorepo but there are potentially other ways to do it.  This will setup your packages and services workspace</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;my-monorepo&quot;,
  &quot;private&quot;: true,
  &quot;workspaces&quot;: [&quot;packages/*&quot;, &quot;services/*&quot;]
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now update the path in next-i18next.config.js, you can use Yarn's Workspace feature to resolve the correct path to your language packs:</p>
<pre><code>const path = require(&quot;path&quot;);
const { workspaces } = require(&quot;../../package.json&quot;); // Adjust the path to your root package.json

module.exports = {
  i18n: {
    locales: [&quot;en&quot;, &quot;ko&quot;],
    defaultLocale: &quot;en&quot;,
    localeDetection: false,
    defaultNS: &quot;common&quot;,
    localePath: path.resolve(
      __dirname, `../../${workspaces.packages}/i18n/locales` // Adjust the path based on your monorepo setup
    ),
    debug: true,
  },
};
</code></pre>
<p>From there it should hopefully be resolved; you may need to fiddle with the individual paths a bit, the main part is ensuring workspaces.packages is correct.  I've included an additional monorepo beginner <a href="https://medium.com/swlh/yarn-workspaces-monorepo-beginners-guide-ed89de47aa25" rel="nofollow noreferrer">guide</a> that will include more examples and help with this and an additional simple <a href="https://github.com/pedronauck/yarn-workspaces-example" rel="nofollow noreferrer">example</a>.</p>

Q: Auth in chrome extension with supabase A: <p>Trying to share localStorage between your web app and the extension will not work. Browser security blocks extensions from reading another origin's localStorage, and even a content script added to your web app cannot access it directly.</p>
<p>A better solution is to run Supabase auth directly inside the extension. This is easier than it sounds once you handle a few MV3-specific issues. Here is what I did:</p>
<p>Supabase saves sessions in localStorage by default, but MV3 service workers do not have localStorage. The default OAuth flow puts the token in the URL hash (#access_token=...), but Chrome.identity.launchWebAuthFlow strips the hash before returning. You need PKCE flow so the code arrives as ?code=... in the query string instead.</p>
<p>1: Create a chrome.storage.local adapter</p>
<p>Replace Supabase’s default storage with one that uses chrome.storage.local. You will also need a write through memory cache so synchronous reads work.</p>
<p>This makes getItem() reads work in the service worker:</p>
<pre><code>import { createClient } from &quot;@supabase/supabase-js&quot;;

let _cache = {};

const chromeStorageAdapter = {
  getItem: (key) =&gt; _cache[key] ?? null,
  setItem: (key, value) =&gt; {
    _cache[key] = value;
    chrome.storage.local.set({ [key]: value });
  },
  removeItem: (key) =&gt; {
    delete _cache[key];
    chrome.storage.local.remove(key);
  },
};

export async function hydrateStorageCache() {
  return new Promise((resolve) =&gt; {
    chrome.storage.local.get(null, (items) =&gt; {
      _cache = { ..._cache, ...items };
      resolve();
    });
  });
}

export const supabase = createClient(
  &quot;https://YOUR_PROJECT.supabase.co&quot;,
  &quot;YOUR_ANON_KEY&quot;,
  {
    auth: {
      storage: chromeStorageAdapter,
      autoRefreshToken: true,
      persistSession: true,
      detectSessionInUrl: false,
      flowType: &quot;pkce&quot;,
    },
  }
);
</code></pre>
<p>2: The login function</p>
<pre><code>import { supabase } from &quot;./supabaseClient.js&quot;;

const REDIRECT_URL = chrome.identity.getRedirectURL();

export async function loginWithGoogle() {
  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth({
    provider: &quot;google&quot;,
    options: {
      redirectTo: REDIRECT_URL,
      skipBrowserRedirect: true,
    },
  });

  if (error) throw error;

  const redirectResult = await new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {
    chrome.identity.launchWebAuthFlow(
      { url: data.url, interactive: true },
      (url) =&gt;
        chrome.runtime.lastError
          ? reject(new Error(chrome.runtime.lastError.message))
          : resolve(url)
    );
  });

  const code = new URL(redirectResult).searchParams.get(&quot;code&quot;);

  if (!code) throw new Error(&quot;No code returned&quot;);

  const { data: session } =
    await supabase.auth.exchangeCodeForSession(code);

  return session;
}
</code></pre>
<p>3: Background service worker</p>
<pre><code>import { hydrateStorageCache, supabase } from &quot;./supabaseClient.js&quot;;
import { loginWithGoogle } from &quot;./auth.js&quot;;

chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(async () =&gt; {
  await hydrateStorageCache();
});

chrome.runtime.onStartup.addListener(async () =&gt; {
  await hydrateStorageCache();
});

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((msg, _sender, sendResponse) =&gt; {
  if (msg.action === &quot;login&quot;) {
    loginWithGoogle()
      .then(sendResponse)
      .catch((e) =&gt; sendResponse({ error: e.message }));
  }

  if (msg.action === &quot;get-session&quot;) {
    supabase.auth
      .getSession()
      .then(({ data }) =&gt; sendResponse(data.session));
  }

  if (msg.action === &quot;logout&quot;) {
    supabase.auth.signOut().then(sendResponse);
  }

  return true;
});
</code></pre>
<p>In your popup, use this code:</p>
<pre><code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: &quot;login&quot; }, (session) =&gt;
  console.log(session)
);
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>4: manifest.json</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;permissions&quot;: [&quot;storage&quot;, &quot;identity&quot;],
  &quot;host_permissions&quot;: [&quot;https://*.supabase.co/*&quot;],
  &quot;background&quot;: {
    &quot;service_worker&quot;: &quot;background.js&quot;,
    &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>5: Dashboard configuration (this part is easy to get wrong)</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Supabase → Auth → Providers → Google: enable it and add your Google Client ID + Secret</p>
</li>
<li><p>Supabase → Auth → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs: add https://.chromiumapp.org/</p>
</li>
<li><p>Google Cloud Console → Authorized redirect URIs: add <a href="https://your_project.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://YOUR_PROJECT.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback</a> (this is Supabase's callback, not the extension URL. People often mix these up.)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>If you see &quot;Authorization page could not be loaded,&quot; it almost always means either (a) the Google provider is not enabled in the Supabase dashboard yet, or (b) the redirect URL is not in Supabase's allowed list. Check the service worker console for the OAuth URL and paste it into a browser tab to see the exact error Supabase returns.</p>
<p>Once everything is set up, the session will persist across service worker restarts because it uses chrome.storage.local instead of memory.</p>
<p>Source: <a href="https://medium.com/@chethiyakd/supabase-auth-in-a-chrome-extension-what-you-wont-find-in-the-docs-a2ae6691cca3" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://medium.com/@chethiyakd/supabase-auth-in-a-chrome-extension-what-you-wont-find-in-the-docs-a2ae6691cca3</a></p>

Q: Trim String/Text in Flutter A: <p>As you said in the comments, a trim approach won't work here because you don't know how much to trim off. The ID portion of the URL may not be guaranteed to always be three characters.</p>
<p>Instead of trying to trim the parts of the URL you don't want, use a regular expression to match only the parts that you <strong>do</strong> want:</p>
<p>(<a href="https://regex101.com/r/vfu67R/1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Regex101</a>)</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>final url = 'http://sales.local/api/v1/payments/454/ticket/verify?token=jhvycygvjhbknm.eyJpc3MiOiJodH';
final pattern = RegExp(r'^http:\/\/sales\.local\/api\/v1\/payments\/(\d+)');
final match = pattern.firstMatch(url);

final shortUrl = match?.group(0);
final id = match?.group(1);

print(shortUrl); // &quot;http://sales.local/api/v1/payments/454&quot;
print(id);       // &quot;454&quot;
</code></pre>
